# FPGA Video Processing and RK3588 Domestic Compute: Two High-Value Application Directions

> Author: Xi'an Qiyun Zhixun Electronic Technology Co., Ltd.
> Keywords: FPGA · Video stitching · SDI/HDMI · RK3588 · Domestic/localized compute · Edge computing

In embedded hardware, two directions have stayed hot in recent years: **audio/video processing** — command & dispatch, broadcast, and conference systems will always need low-latency, high-throughput video solutions — and **domestic compute platforms**, where IT localization (Xinchuang) and supply-chain security have pushed RK3588 and other Chinese SoCs into the spotlight. Qiyun Zhixun has volume-deployed products in both directions. This article walks through how to choose platforms and what it takes to ship, based on real cases.

## 1. FPGA Video Processing: Low Latency Is a Hard Requirement

### Why video applications favor FPGA

Video processing is a classic FPGA domain. Compared with general-purpose processors, FPGA's parallel architecture is a natural fit for pixel-level parallel processing of multiple video streams; compared with dedicated ASICs, FPGAs are reconfigurable and can adapt flexibly to different protocols and algorithm evolution. For scenarios like command & dispatch, **latency is a safety metric** — millisecond-level delays can affect decisions, and FPGA's deterministic processing advantage is irreplaceable.

### Case 1: 4K/8K Multi-Screen Video Stitching Processor

- **Architecture**: Xilinx FPGA
- **Capability**: supports 16 video inputs with multi-screen stitching
- **Key metric**: processing latency below 1 frame
- **Deployed in**: command & dispatch centers and other venues requiring synchronized multi-source display on large screens

The difficulty of multi-screen stitching lies in **synchronization** and **bandwidth**: 16 inputs must stay frame-synchronized, and the pixel throughput of 4K/8K resolutions demands sufficient DDR bandwidth and carefully designed pipelines. From FPGA selection and DDR routing to logic implementation, such designs require a team experienced in high-speed board design.

### Case 2: Professional AV Conversion and Streaming Devices

- SDI/HDMI format converters — mixed SDI/HDMI environments are the norm in broadcast; conversion must preserve color space and timing without loss
- 4K live-streaming encoders — low-latency encoding and stable streaming for live broadcast
- 32-channel digital audio processors — routing, EQ, and delay processing for multi-channel audio

### Trend: From Format Conversion to Intelligent Stitching

The evolution of FPGA video solutions is clear: early work did format conversion, mid-stage work did real-time multi-screen stitching, and now AI-assisted analysis is being layered on. The stack is moving from "pure logic" to "logic + algorithms", which demands teams with combined FPGA and algorithm expertise.

## 2. RK3588 Domestic Compute: Full-Stack Capability from Chip to System

### Why RK3588

With an 8-core high-performance CPU, 8K video encode/decode, and rich peripheral interfaces, RK3588 has become a popular choice for domestic embedded compute — from industrial gateways and edge AI terminals to digital signage and smart cockpits. But turning a bare BGA chip into a mass-producible system sits behind a chain of engineering work: core board design, power, thermal, and wide-temperature validation.

### Qiyun Zhixun's RK3588 Full-Stack Domestic Compute Platform

Targeting industrial control, onboard display, rail transit, security AI, and domestic IT (Xinchuang) scenarios, Qiyun Zhixun offers a full series of RK3588 modular compute platforms:

