**9 products** across the three form factors, supporting 8K video decode, with operating temperature covering **-40~85°C** — the latter is a hard requirement for industrial and rail transit use.

### Design Essentials from Chip to System

1. **BGA placement and power design**: RK3588 has high power density; power tree design, decoupling, and thermal paths must be considered together;
2. **High-speed interface integrity**: the impedance and routing quality of PCIe, USB, HDMI/DP and other high-speed signals determine stability;
3. **Wide-temperature validation**: -40~85°C means component selection, soldering processes, and thermal design must all follow industrial-grade standards;
4. **Software ecosystem**: BSP, drivers, and OS bring-up must ship together with the hardware so customers can use it out of the box.

## 3. Platform Selection Guide: FPGA, RK3588, or Heterogeneous?

| Scenario | Recommended Platform | Rationale |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Multi-stream parallel video, low latency, changing protocols | FPGA | Parallelism + deterministic latency |
| General compute, AI inference, Linux ecosystem, localization | RK3588 | Compute + ecosystem + Xinchuang compliance |
| Need both high-speed parallelism and general compute | Heterogeneous (e.g., Zynq SoC) | FPGA logic + ARM general compute complement each other |

Qiyun Zhixun has shipped cases on all three — FPGA for video stitching and industrial vision (Zynq + GigE Vision + AI inference acceleration), ARM for the full RK3588 series. During platform selection, asking "how mass-producible is this solution?" is often more valuable than debating spec sheets.

